WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.980
A Porsche with a hybrid system!

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.440
And a Porsche with four seats!

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.740
The Panamera has already dished out its share
of surprises for sports car fans.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:17.810
Now, for the second generation, the Panamera
4 plug-in E-hybrid has been joined by another

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:21.800
with 500 kilowatts of system power.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.050
But for today, we'll stick with the less powerful
plug-in-hybrid.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:33.740
Its electric motor and 243-kilowatt V6 gasoline
engine add up to a total system power of 340 kilowatts.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:39.180
Fuel consumption is rated at just 2.5
liters per 100 kilometers, but only as long

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.860
as the driver resists the temptation to really step on it.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:50.179
Our tester Reinhold Deisenhofer points out
that, since the Panamera E-hybrid weighs 2.2 tons,

00:00:50.179 --> 00:00:55.469
it takes some power to push that weight
from zero to 100 kilometers per hour in just

00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:59.649
five seconds, but the system has a good 700
newtonmeters of torque.

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:04.559
The Normal drive mode recharges the battery
on the go, and then there are the Sport and

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:06.030
Sportplus modes.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:11.020
In all three, the overboost function temporarily
steps up the boost pressure for the pick-up

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.840
typical of Porsche.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.620
As in most hybrids, the display can show the
energy flow at any time.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.440
A Porsche is a Porsche is a Porsche.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.220
The design has spoken the same easily recognizeable
language for decades, and the Panamera is

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:40.990
no exception.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:47.299
The E-hybrid differs from the 4-seater's gasoline-powered version only in small details - such as the

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.600
green-rimmed badging and the green brake calipers.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.079
The Panamera 4 E-hybrid is 5 meters and 5
centimeters long.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.940
The longer executive body style is available
on request.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.460
Tucked under the front hood is the V6 with
2.9 liters capacity.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:34.320
Reinhold Deisenhofer realizes that, with a
Porsche, sound is always a factor.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:39.050
In all-electric drive, the E-hybrid glides
over the asphalt on its air suspension in

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:40.860
almost total silence.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.000
But if you really bring out the performance,
it sounds just like the Porsches we've always known.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:02.600
The Panamera E-hybrid pounces from 80 to 120
kilometers per hour in just 3.1 seconds

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:07.700
- only a fraction of a second slower than
other Panamera variants - because of the few

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.900
hundred extra kilos the battery adds.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:28.980
Reinhold explains that it takes
about 6 hours to fully recharge the e-hybrid's

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.660
battery from a European 220-volt household
outlet.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:37.660
A quick-charge station for an extra four and
a half thousand euros will cut two hours off

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:38.660
that time.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:46.310
The Panamera E-hybrid's competitors are the
Mercedes Benz S500 and the BMW 7 seriesr as plug-in-hybrids,

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:53.659
but in the family of Panameras, the Porsche e-Hybrid is actually the lowest priced of them all.

